<p>San Antonio's creeks can go from dry to dangerous in under an hour. Intense rain falls on thin soils and limestone hills to the north and west, runs off quickly, and pours into Leon, Salado, Olmos, and dozens of smaller creeks that wind through neighborhoods. That geography is why flash flood damage and insurance questions come up here more than many homeowners expect.</p>

<p>The hardest part of a flood-related claim is often not the cleanup. It is figuring out which policy, if any, covers the damage. This guide explains how flood insurance differs from water damage coverage, how NFIP claims generally work, and what to do after water gets in. It is general information, not legal advice; your policy language controls.</p>

<h2>Why San Antonio is in Flash Flood Alley</h2>

<p>The Balcones Escarpment runs along the northern and western edge of San Antonio, where the Texas Hill Country drops to the coastal plains. Moist air from the Gulf is pushed up against that rise, which can fuel very heavy rainfall. Runoff from the hills then funnels into creeks that cross the city.</p>

<p>The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database records 420 flash flood reports and 45 river or areal flood reports in Bexar County. Some of the most significant events:</p>

