<p>The June 2025 event was a tragedy, with multiple lives lost in the Beitel Creek area. Life safety always comes first: never drive or walk into floodwater, and pay attention to low water crossing closures.</p>

<p>Look closely at the NOAA narratives and a pattern appears. Many reports describe low water crossings closed across northern and western Bexar County, creeks such as Olmos, Salado, Leon, and Beitel running out of their banks, and water rescues on busy roads. Flooding is not limited to homes beside a river. Neighborhoods in Leon Valley, Live Oak, Alamo Heights, Stone Oak, and the east side all appear in the record. If a creek or drainage channel runs near your street, your home may face some flood risk even if it has never flooded before.</p>

<h2>Flood vs. water damage: why the difference matters</h2>

<p>Insurers draw a sharp line between "flood" and other water damage. Which side of that line your damage falls on often determines which policy responds.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Usually handled under</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Creek overflows or street water flows into the house</td><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private)</td><td>Homeowners policies generally exclude flood and surface water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heavy rain pools around the slab and seeps in</td><td>Flood policy, if the definition of flood is met</td><td>Often excluded under homeowners policies as surface water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind or hail opens the roof and rain pours in</td><td>Homeowners or commercial policy</td><td>Typically treated as part of the wind or hail loss, subject to the wind/hail deductible</td></tr>
<tr><td>A supply line or pipe bursts inside the home</td><td>Homeowners or commercial policy</td><td>Sudden and accidental discharge is commonly covered; long-term leaks often are not</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sewer or drain backs up during a storm</td><td>Depends on endorsement</td><td>Many policies require a water backup endorsement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slow leak over months</td><td>Often excluded</td><td>Wear, deterioration, and repeated seepage exclusions are common</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A real San Antonio storm can create more than one of these at once. A severe thunderstorm may peel back shingles and also send water from a nearby creek into the garage. In that case, the homeowners insurer may owe for the roof and interior rain damage, while the flood insurer handles rising water. Careful documentation of where water entered is what separates the two.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Policy-Analysis.webp" alt="Two people discussing documents together at a laptop" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reading the flood exclusion and any water endorsements in your policy is the first step after water damage.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How flood insurance works</h2>

<h3>NFIP and private flood policies</h3>

<p>Most flood insurance in Texas is written through the National Flood Insurance Program, managed by FEMA, and sold through insurance agents. Private flood policies are also available. According to FloodSmart.gov, NFIP residential policies offer building coverage up to $250,000 and contents coverage up to $100,000, purchased separately. Private policies may offer different limits and terms.</p>

<h3>The 30-day waiting period</h3>

<p>NFIP policies typically take effect 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions. If heavy rain is in the forecast, it is usually too late to buy coverage for that event. Review your flood risk and coverage well before storm season.</p>

<h3>What NFIP typically covers and limits</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Building coverage</strong> generally includes the structure, its foundation, electrical and plumbing systems, HVAC equipment, water heaters, built-in appliances, and permanently installed flooring and cabinets.</li>
<li><strong>Contents coverage</strong> is separate and covers personal belongings, often at actual cash value under the standard NFIP dwelling form.</li>
<li><strong>Limits apply</strong> to items below the lowest elevated floor and in basements. Most San Antonio homes are slab-on-grade, but some older pier-and-beam homes in neighborhoods like King William and Monte Vista have crawlspaces with their own coverage questions.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses</strong> are generally not covered by NFIP policies, unlike many homeowners policies.</li>
</ul>

<p>Read your own Standard Flood Insurance Policy or private flood policy for details. Coverage descriptions here are general.</p>

