<h2>Filing a flood claim: practical steps</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Wait until authorities say it is safe to return. Do not enter standing water that may be energized. Contact CPS Energy about gas or electrical hazards.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> to your flood insurer, and to your homeowners insurer if wind, hail, or other covered damage may also be involved.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before cleanup, including the high-water line on walls, outside and inside.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the water depth</strong> in each room and note it in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Keep samples</strong> of damaged flooring, carpet, and drywall if practical, and list everything you throw away.</li>
<li><strong>Dry out quickly.</strong> Mold can develop fast in South Texas heat and humidity. Remove wet materials and keep receipts for fans, dehumidifiers, and mitigation services.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the proof of loss deadline.</strong> NFIP claims require a signed proof of loss within the time set by the policy (generally 60 days after the loss unless FEMA extends it). Missing it can jeopardize payment.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> includes a contents inventory section that works for flood claims, too.</p>

<h2>When the homeowners policy does apply</h2>

<p>Not every water loss during a storm is a flood. If wind or hail damaged your roof, windows, or siding and rain came through those openings, that interior damage is usually part of a wind or hail claim under your homeowners policy. Similarly, a pipe that bursts during a freeze is a <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claim</a>, not a flood claim. See our guide on <a href="/freeze-damage-burst-pipe-claims-san-antonio/">freeze damage and burst pipe claims in San Antonio</a> for that scenario.</p>

<p>Insurers sometimes deny storm-related water damage as "flood" or "surface water" when the water actually came through a damaged roof. If that happens, photos of the roof opening, the water path, and the absence of a high-water line can matter. Our page on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> explains next steps.</p>

<h2>Rebuilding after a flood</h2>

<p>Substantial flood damage can trigger building and floodplain requirements. In the City of San Antonio, repairs generally need permits from the Development Services Department, and properties in a mapped floodplain may face additional rules about elevation. NFIP policies include Increased Cost of Compliance coverage, which may help with certain required mitigation costs after a substantially damaged building; FloodSmart.gov describes the requirements. For more on how codes interact with insurance, read about <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-antonio/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Antonio</a>.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Policy deadlines are strict</span><p>Flood policies, especially NFIP policies, have firm deadlines for proof of loss and for disputing a decision. Lawsuit deadlines are separate and may differ. If you have questions about legal deadlines or rights, consult a Texas attorney.</p></div>

<h2>Preparing before the next flash flood</h2>

<p>Flash floods give little warning, so most of the useful work happens before the rain. A few steps can make a future claim far easier:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check your flood risk.</strong> Look up your property on FEMA's flood maps and ask your agent about NFIP and private flood options. Remember that flooding in San Antonio has reached homes outside mapped high-risk areas.</li>
<li><strong>Review your homeowners policy.</strong> Find the flood and surface water exclusions and see whether you have a water backup endorsement. Note your deductibles.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph your home and belongings.</strong> A walk-through video of every room, closets and garage included, is one of the best tools for a contents claim.</li>
<li><strong>Store documents safely.</strong> Keep digital copies of your policies, receipts for major purchases, and your agent's contact information somewhere you can reach from a phone.</li>
<li><strong>Know the history.</strong> Our <a href="/storm-history/">Bexar County storm history</a> lets you see flood reports near your neighborhood.</li>
</ul>

<p>Businesses should also review business income and extra expense coverage, which may or may not respond to flood depending on the policy. Our page on <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> covers those issues in more detail.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Flood losses often involve hundreds of damaged items, moisture-damaged building materials, and sometimes two insurers with different rules. San Antonio Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps homeowners and businesses with <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> by documenting the loss, preparing the building estimate and contents inventory, and helping keep the proof of loss and other deadlines on track. When wind and flood overlap, separating the damage clearly can make both claims easier to evaluate. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Outcomes always depend on the policy and the facts.</p>

